   "name": "Heat transfer: radiation"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Radiation is the transfer of heat as invisible rays (infrared waves) from a hot body, without needing any material. It can travel through empty space, like the heat of the sun reaching the Earth.\nSurfaces and radiation:\n• dull, dark (black) surfaces absorb and give out radiation well;\n• shiny, light (white or silver) surfaces reflect radiation and absorb little.\nExamples in the kitchen: grilling and toasting (heat rays from the grill or charcoal reach the food); roasting plantain or corn by an open fire; feeling the heat of a fire on your face; sun-drying fish, cocoa and cassava.\nAdvantages: quick cooking (grilling); food gets an attractive brown colour; free solar energy for drying.\nDisadvantages: food can burn on the outside before the inside is cooked; heat is lost from hot pots; a cook near the fire gets very hot.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "the outside of shiny cooking pots are good for keeping food hot"
  },
  "problem": "Explain why {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "Shiny surfaces give out (radiate) little heat.",
   "So less heat is lost from the pot by radiation."
  ],
  "answer": "Shiny surfaces are poor radiators, so the food stays hot longer.",
  "checks": []
 },
